F={1:[("1.4","ECB vs. CBC","ECB encrypts each block independently, so identical blocks leak patterns; CBC XORs each block with the previous ciphertext (starting from an IV)."),
("1.4","GCM","Galois/Counter Mode: counter-mode encryption with built-in authentication &mdash; confidentiality and integrity together."),
("1.4","3DES, IDEA, RC4","Legacy algorithms: 3DES and IDEA are old block ciphers; RC4 is a broken stream cipher (WEP, old TLS)."),
("1.4","XOR","Exclusive or: 1 when bits differ, 0 when they match. Reversible, so it's the core of stream ciphers and CBC."),
("1.4","Registration authority (RA)","Verifies a requester's identity before the CA issues a certificate."),
("1.4","PKCS #12 / .p12 / .pfx","A password-protected file holding a certificate and its private key."),
("1.4","PEM vs. DER","The same certificate as Base64 text (PEM) or binary (DER)."),
("1.4","KEK","Key encryption key: encrypts other keys rather than data."),
("1.4","DSA vs. ECDSA","Digital signature algorithms; ECDSA uses elliptic curves for much smaller keys. Neither encrypts."),
("1.4","CRC","Checksum that detects accidental errors only &mdash; not tamper protection."),
("1.4","Volume vs. partition vs. record encryption","One logical volume; one partition of a drive; individual database rows each with their own key."),
("1.2","MDF / IDF","Main and intermediate distribution frames &mdash; wiring closets that need physical access control.")],
2:[("2.4","Malicious code (network attack)","Code spread or delivered across the network: worms exploiting services, malicious scripts, backdoors, botnet C2."),
("2.3","ASLR","Address space layout randomization: random memory addresses so exploits can't predict where to jump."),
("2.3","DEP","Data execution prevention: marks data memory non-executable so injected code can't run."),
("2.2","PBX / VoIP risks","Phone systems: caller-ID spoofing for vishing, toll fraud, eavesdropping. Encrypt media with SRTP."),
("2.4","WPS weakness","The Wi-Fi Protected Setup PIN is checked in two halves and can be brute-forced; disable WPS."),
("2.4","RTBH","Remotely triggered black hole: the ISP drops traffic to the attacked address via BGP. Stops a DDoS, but takes the target offline."),
("2.4","Published/documented indicator","Evidence of your breach appears publicly &mdash; leaked data, attacker boasts, press reports &mdash; often before you notice."),
("2.1","War (motivation)","Nation-state cyber attacks as part of armed conflict, against military or critical infrastructure.")],
3:[("3.2","AH vs. ESP","IPSec Authentication Header gives integrity and authentication only; Encapsulating Security Payload adds encryption."),
("3.2","IKE","Internet Key Exchange: negotiates IPSec keys and security associations using Diffie-Hellman."),
("3.2","GRE / L2TP / PPTP","GRE and L2TP tunnel without encrypting (paired with IPSec); PPTP is broken."),
("3.2","CASB","Cloud access security broker: visibility and control over cloud app use, including shadow IT and DLP."),
("3.2","SWG","Secure web gateway: proxy that filters web traffic &mdash; URL filtering, malware scanning."),
("3.2","PAP vs. CHAP","Cleartext password vs. hashed challenge-response."),
("3.2","BPDU guard","Shuts an access port that receives spanning-tree BPDUs, stopping rogue switches and loops."),
("3.2","WIDS / WIPS","Wireless IDS/IPS: detect (and contain) rogue APs, evil twins and deauth floods."),
("3.1","IaaS vs. PaaS vs. SaaS","Customer manages OS and up vs. just code and data vs. just data and users."),
("3.1","VPC","Virtual private cloud: an isolated network inside a public cloud."),
("3.1","MSSP","Managed security service provider: outsourced security operations such as a 24/7 SOC."),
("3.1","VDI","Virtual desktop infrastructure: desktops run in the data centre and are streamed; data never lands on the device."),
("3.4","RAID 1 / 5 / 6 / 10","Mirror; parity, survives one disk; double parity, survives two; mirrored stripes. RAID 0 has no redundancy."),
("3.4","RAID vs. backup","RAID survives a disk failure; only backups recover from deletion, corruption and ransomware.")],
4:[("4.6","TOTP vs. HOTP","Time-based code that changes every ~30 s vs. counter-based code that changes each time it's used."),
("4.6","Kerberos KDC and TGT","The key distribution center issues a ticket granting ticket at login, exchanged for service tickets. Needs synced clocks."),
("4.6","NTLM","Legacy Windows challenge-response authentication; pass-the-hash target. Prefer Kerberos."),
("4.6","PIV / CAC","Government and military smart cards holding a certificate, used with a PIN."),
("4.1","WEP / WPA-TKIP","Both use the broken RC4 cipher; never use them. WPA2 (AES-CCMP) at minimum, WPA3 preferred."),
("4.1","COBO","Corporate-owned, business only: no personal use."),
("4.1","UEFI Secure Boot","Firmware check that only a signed boot loader runs, blocking bootkits."),
("4.1","OWASP","Open Worldwide Application Security Project &mdash; publishes the OWASP Top 10 web risks."),
("4.3","STIX vs. TAXII","The format for describing threat intel vs. the transport for sharing it."),
("4.3","MITRE ATT&amp;CK","Knowledge base of attacker tactics and techniques (TTPs), used for detection mapping and threat hunting."),
("4.3","AIS","CISA's Automated Indicator Sharing program, using STIX/TAXII."),
("4.5","PAT vs. DNAT","Many internal hosts share one public IP vs. a public port forwarded to one internal server."),
("4.5","SRTP","Secure Real-time Transport Protocol: encrypted voice and video."),
("4.5","S/MIME","Certificate-based encryption and signing of individual email messages."),
("4.5","TSIG","Shared-key authentication of DNS zone transfers and updates.")],
5:[("5.2","Neutral risk appetite","Balanced: accepts moderate risk when the expected benefit roughly matches the potential loss."),
("5.5","Examination","A formal, in-depth review by an outside authority such as a regulator, often on site."),
("5.2","MTTF","Mean time to failure: expected life of a part that is replaced, not repaired."),
("5.2","ROI (security)","A control is worth it when the loss it prevents (ALE reduction) exceeds its cost."),
("5.1","CISO / ISSO","Owns the security program vs. security of a specific system."),
("5.1","DPO","Data protection officer: oversees personal-data handling; required under GDPR for many organizations."),
("5.3","EULA","End user license agreement: the terms accepted to use software.")]}
Q={1:[("1.4","Which block cipher mode leaks patterns because identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext?",["CBC","GCM","ECB","CFB"],2,"ECB encrypts every block independently with the same key, so repeating data stays visible."),
("1.4","A file must carry a certificate together with its private key, protected by a password, to move it to another server. Which format fits?",["PEM","DER","PKCS #12 (.pfx/.p12)","CSR"],2,"PKCS #12 bundles the certificate and private key in one password-protected file.")],
2:[("2.3","Which protection randomizes where programs and libraries load in memory so exploits can't predict addresses?",["DEP","ASLR","SEH","TPM"],1,"ASLR randomizes addresses; DEP stops data memory from executing."),
("2.4","A worm spreads between Windows servers through a vulnerable SMB service with no user interaction. Which 2.4 category best fits?",["Physical attack","Malicious code (network attack)","Password attack","Replay attack"],1,"Code delivered and spread across the network is the malicious code network attack.")],
3:[("3.2","Which IPSec protocol provides encryption?",["AH","ESP","IKE","GRE"],1,"ESP encrypts and authenticates; AH only authenticates; IKE negotiates keys; GRE is a separate unencrypted tunnel."),
("3.1","A company deploys its own code to a provider-managed platform and never touches the OS. Which service model is this?",["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],1,"PaaS: the provider manages everything up to the runtime; the customer manages code and data."),
("3.4","Why isn't RAID 5 a backup?",["It's too slow","It copies deletion and ransomware to every disk instantly","It needs four disks","It only works with SSDs"],1,"RAID protects against a disk failing, not against bad changes to the data.")],
4:[("4.6","An authenticator app shows a new six-digit code every 30 seconds. Which algorithm is it using?",["HOTP","TOTP","NTLM","SAML"],1,"Time-based codes are TOTP; counter-based codes that change on use are HOTP."),
("4.3","Which pair describes threat intelligence (format) and moves it between organizations (transport)?",["TAXII and STIX","STIX and TAXII","ATT&CK and AIS","OVAL and SCAP"],1,"STIX is the format, TAXII the transport."),
("4.6","Users suddenly can't log in to the domain, and investigation shows the domain controller's clock is 20 minutes off. Which protocol is affected?",["NTLM","Kerberos","RADIUS","LDAP"],1,"Kerberos tickets are time-stamped, so clock skew breaks authentication.")],
5:[("5.2","An organization accepts moderate risk when the expected benefit roughly equals the potential loss. What is its risk appetite?",["Expansionary","Conservative","Neutral","Exempt"],2,"Neutral sits between expansionary (seeks risk for growth) and conservative (avoids it)."),
("5.5","Banking regulators arrive on site to inspect records and controls, and their findings carry enforcement weight. What is this?",["Self-assessment","Examination","Attestation","Tabletop exercise"],1,"An examination is a formal, in-depth review by an outside authority.")]}
